---
import dark from '../../../docs/screenshots/list-dark.png';
import light from '../../../docs/screenshots/list-light.png';
import onboarding from '../../../docs/screenshots/onboarding.png';
import Screenshot from '../components/Screenshot.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { page, RELEASES, REPO, STORE } from '../links';
---

<Base
  title="Install: Prowl"
  description="Install Prowl from the Chrome Web Store in a minute, or load a release zip as an unpacked extension. Then pin it, open the side panel and sign in."
>
  <article class="section wrap prose guide">
    <h1>Install Prowl</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Install Prowl from the Chrome Web Store: it takes a minute and Prowl updates itself. You need
      no account besides GitHub.
    </p>
    <p class="actions">
      <a class="button" data-variant="primary" href={STORE}>Add to Chrome</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Chrome 116 or newer. Prowl is built for and tested on Chrome.</li>
      <li>A GitHub account.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Steps</h2>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Add Prowl to Chrome</h3>
        <p>
          Open <a href={STORE}>Prowl in the Chrome Web Store</a> and choose
          <strong>Add to Chrome</strong>. Chrome lists what Prowl may access; the
          <a href={page('privacy/')}>privacy page</a> explains each permission.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Pin it</h3>
        <p>
          Click the puzzle-piece Extensions button in the toolbar, then the pin next to Prowl, so
          its icon stays in reach.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Open the side panel and sign in</h3>
        <p>
          Click the Prowl icon. The side panel opens on the sign-in screen. Choose
          <strong>Continue with GitHub</strong>, or paste a token. The
          <a href={page('auth/')}>sign-in guide</a> explains both and the exact scopes.
        </p>
        <Screenshot
          light={onboarding}
          alt="Prowl's sign-in screen with the Continue with GitHub button and a field for a personal access token."
        />
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      That is all. Prowl lists your pull requests and keeps them up to date, with the panel open or
      closed.
    </p>
    <Screenshot
      light={light}
      dark={dark}
      alt="The Prowl side panel listing pull requests with their CI, review and merge status."
    />

    <h2>Update</h2>
    <p>
      Chrome updates Prowl from the store by itself. A new version reaches the store a few days
      after its <a href={RELEASES}>GitHub release</a>, once Google has reviewed it.
    </p>

    <h2>Install from a release zip</h2>
    <p>
      To run a release before it reaches the store, or without the store, load the zip yourself.
      Chrome does not update it: repeat these steps for each new release.
    </p>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Download the zip</h3>
        <p>
          Open <a href={RELEASES}>the latest release</a> and download
          <code>prowl-vX.Y.Z.zip</code> from the Assets list. Do not pick the "Source code"
          archives.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Unzip it into a new folder</h3>
        <p>
          Choose a place you will keep. Chrome reads the extension from this folder every time it
          starts, so do not move or delete it. On macOS and Linux, run this where you saved the zip:
        </p>
        <pre><code>unzip prowl-v*.zip -d ~/prowl-extension</code></pre>
        <p>
          On Windows, right-click the zip and choose Extract All. The folder must contain
          <code>manifest.json</code> directly, not inside another folder.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Open the extensions page</h3>
        <p>
          Type <code>chrome://extensions</code> in the address bar. Turn on <strong>Developer
          mode</strong>, the switch at the top right.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Load Prowl</h3>
        <p>
          Choose <strong>Load unpacked</strong> and select the folder from step 2. Prowl appears in
          your list of extensions.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Pin it and sign in</h3>
        <p>As in steps 2 and 3 above.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      To update, unzip the new release over the same folder and select the reload button on
      Prowl's card in <code>chrome://extensions</code>. Use the same folder every time: Chrome ties
      your settings and your sign-in to its location, and a new location starts as a fresh install.
      A zip install and a store install are separate extensions: keep only one.
    </p>

    <h2>If something does not work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>"Manifest file is missing or unreadable"</strong> (zip install): you selected the
        wrong folder.
        Pick the one that holds <code>manifest.json</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Clicking the icon does nothing</strong>: check that Chrome is version 116 or newer
        (<code>chrome://settings/help</code>), and that Prowl is switched on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign-in problems</strong>: see the <a href={page('auth/')}>sign-in guide</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Check the download</h2>
    <p>
      Every release zip comes with a build provenance attestation and an SBOM. To check that a zip
      was built from this repository by its release workflow, use the
      <a href="https://cli.github.com/">GitHub CLI</a>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>gh attestation verify prowl-v*.zip --repo aemard/prowl</code></pre>
    <p>
      The store install can be checked against the same zip, and any release can be rebuilt from
      source to the same bytes: see
      <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/releasing.md#verify-a-release`}>Verify a release</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Uninstall</h2>
    <p>
      Remove Prowl on <code>chrome://extensions</code>; Chrome deletes everything it stored. To end
      its access to GitHub as well, revoke the token (see
      <a href={page('auth/#signing-out-and-revoking-access')}>Signing out and revoking access</a>).
    </p>

    <h2>Build it yourself</h2>
    <p>
      Prefer to build from source? Follow <a href={page('contributing/')}>Contributing</a> and load
      the <code>dist</code> folder in step 4. The code is on <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
